@prefix sensing: <http://example.org/orexis/sensing#> .
@prefix sh:          <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix orexis:          <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix rdfs:        <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix scaling: <http://example.org/orexis/scaling#> .

scaling:SensorIsScaledByExactlyOneShape a sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:comment "Every sensor's raw values are turned into quantities by exactly one scaling, checked on the DERIVED fact. The failure this refuses is quiet in a way the codec one is not: two scalings would not produce a parse error or an empty reading, they would produce a NUMBER — a plausible one, recorded as a measurement, differing from the truth by whatever the losing curve would have done. A reading that is merely wrong is the worst kind to leave to iteration order." ;
    sh:targetClass sosa:Sensor ;
    sh:property [ sh:path scaling:scaledBy ;
                  sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
                  sh:class scaling:Scaling ;
                  sh:message "a sensor must be scaled by exactly one curve — this is derived from scaling:curve, or from its absence" ] .

scaling:OnlyACurveIsStatedShape a sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:comment "What a world states must be a member of the family. `scaling:Linear` and `scaling:TwoPoint` are declared without implementations and are deliberately NOT refused here — a world may name one, and the build says at startup that it cannot serve it, exactly as sensing:Polling does. Declaring a seam has to remain possible." ;
    sh:targetSubjectsOf scaling:curve ;
    sh:property [ sh:path scaling:curve ;
                  sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:class scaling:Scaling ;
                  sh:message "a device follows one curve — scaling:curve takes one member of scaling:Scaling" ] .

scaling:AUnitIsAUnitShape a sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:comment """EXACTLY one unit per sensor. This used to be `maxCount` alone, on the subjects that already stated one, with a comment explaining the gap: only world/sensing stated units, and requiring them would have refused the others for a fact that change did not set out to backfill.

The gap has been paid for since. A dashboard panel needs the unit to know what it is drawing, and a sensor without one fell back to a default — so a soil moisture in one world was rendered correctly and the same reading in another was rendered as a bare number, because nothing said what it was. Both worlds state units now, so the reason for the gap is gone and the requirement can stand.

Targeted at the SENSOR rather than at whatever happens to state the property, which is the other half: a shape hung on `sh:targetSubjectsOf` can only ever check the ones that already comply.""" ;
    sh:targetClass sosa:Sensor ;
    sh:property [ sh:path scaling:quantityUnit ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
                  sh:message "a sensor reads in exactly one unit — state scaling:quantityUnit, or nothing downstream can tell a temperature from a fraction" ] .
